<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">March of Montferrat</h1><p class="st-meta">967 CE – 1574 CE · capital Casale Monferrato</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Marquisate in Piedmont whose Aleramici and Palaiologos rulers were crusaders and kings in the East, raised to a duchy in 1574.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 967 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i-duke-of-bavaria">Otto I</a> confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleramo">Aleramo</a>, a nobleman of Frankish descent, as margrave of lands in the hills south of the Po between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a>, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>. His descendants, the Aleramici, ruled it for more than three centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Aleramici were famed crusaders: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat">Conrad of Montferrat</a> defended <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tyre">Tyre</a> and was elected King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1192, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface I</a> led the Fourth Crusade and became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>. In 1306 the march passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-palaiologos">Theodore I Palaiologos</a>, son of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronikos II</a>, whose family ruled until 1533. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casale-monferrato">Casale Monferrato</a> became the capital in the 15th century. The territory produced grain and wine.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaiologos-dynasty">Palaiologos</a> died in 1533, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> awarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federico-ii-gonzaga-duke-of-mantua">Federico II Gonzaga</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mantua">Mantua</a> in 1536. In 1574 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Maximilian II</a> raised it to a duchy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gonzaga">Gonzaga</a> held it until 1708, when it passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-savoy">Savoy</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/march-of-montferrat.jpg" alt="March of Montferrat" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Montferrat.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>967 CE – 1574 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Casale Monferrato</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aleramo, Boniface I, Theodore I Palaiologos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Piedmontese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Marquisate of Montferrat, Monferrato</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1270&amp;focus=march-of-montferrat" aria-label="March of Montferrat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4881 584 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where March of Montferrat lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5201 840L5218 839M5201 840L5257 840M5201 840L5201 840"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5218 839h0M5257 840h0M5201 840h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5201 840h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5201 840h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Casale Monferrato. <a href="/atlas?year=1270&amp;focus=march-of-montferrat">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
